# Exemptions from the 1000-line file limit enforced by `cargo xtask max-lines`.
#
# One glob per line; `#` starts a comment. `*` stays inside one path segment, `**` crosses
# segments, and a pattern with no `/` matches that file name at any depth.
#
# Two sections, and the difference between them is the whole point:
#
#   [silent]  Output whose length is a function of what it describes rather than of how well it
#             is written: generated, vendored or lock-like files nobody hand-edits, AND PROSE,
#             where a line budget would be a budget on evidence. No output.
#   [warn]    Hand-written files over the limit with a split already agreed. Prints WARN
#             on every run and does NOT fail - visible debt, not an exemption.
#
# What cannot be listed here at all: anything under `crates/` or `xtask/`. The gate
# rejects such a pattern and fails, because an exemption list that can swallow first-party
# source is a gate that has quietly stopped gating. Split the file instead.
#
# AN EXEMPTION THAT EXEMPTS NOTHING FAILS THE GATE, and it is a rule rather than a request here
# because this file is a list of CLAIMS: the paragraph beside an entry is the only thing a reviewer
# reads to decide whether it is still earned, and it is the paragraph that rots. Two shapes are
# refused, both scoped to entries with no `*` or `?` - a glob over a generated tree legitimately
# covers files that may or may not be long today:
#
#   * a literal path naming no file in the tree - renamed or deleted, exemption outlived it;
#   * a `[warn]` literal whose file is back UNDER the cap - the promise was kept, so the entry
#     stopped printing and stopped being a promise. Delete it and its argument together.
#
# A `[silent]` literal under the cap is NOT refused, and that is measured rather than lenient:
# `devenv.lock` is 86 lines and `flake.lock` 98, and both are right - a lockfile's length is a
# function of the dependency graph and may cross the cap on any day.

[silent]

# Lockfiles. Their length is a function of the dependency graph, and they are regenerated
# by their own tools rather than edited.
Cargo.lock
devenv.lock
flake.lock
pixi.lock

# Dumped contracts: the MCP tool schemas and the OpenAPI spec, once they exist. Their length
# will be a function of the wire types they are derived from.
#
# NOTHING MATCHES THIS PATTERN TODAY, and the comment here used to claim otherwise - it said the
# files are "written by `cargo xtask dump-schemas` from the `schemars` derives and byte-compared
# in CI", and none of those three things exists: no `docs/generated/` directory, no such xtask
# subcommand, no `schemars` in `Cargo.toml` or `Cargo.lock`. Kept as a pattern rather than deleted
# because the exemption should be in place before the first dump lands, but a false present tense
# in an exemption list is how a reader concludes a gate is watching something it is not.
docs/generated/*

# The API reference pages, written by `nix run .#api-docs` from rustdoc JSON and byte-compared
# by `cargo xtask check-api-docs`. Their length is a function of how much public surface a crate
# has and how well it is documented, so a line budget here would be a budget on doc comments.
# Editing one by hand is already caught: every page carries a generated-file header and the gate
# regenerates and compares.
docs/api/*.md

# Decision records. An ADR's length is a function of the decision it carries - the options weighed,
# the measurements that decided it, and what was tried and rejected. Splitting one across files makes
# it LESS reviewable, because a decision has to be readable in one place, and the failure the cap
# exists to catch - a source file accreting responsibilities nobody notices - is not a thing prose
# does. Hand-written and exempt on purpose, which is why the section header above says so.
docs/adr/*.md

# The rest of the published prose, on the SAME reasoning, generalised. The argument that exempts a
# decision record was never specific to decision records: a page's length is a function of what it
# has to explain, and the failure the cap exists to catch is a SOURCE FILE accreting
# responsibilities nobody notices. Prose does not do that - a long page is legible or it is not, and
# a reviewer can see which, whereas a 1200-line module hides its seams.
#
# This was learned the wrong way round rather than reasoned from the start, and the note is here so
# it is not re-learned: the implementation plan was split across two files to get under this cap,
# then a third time, and each split cost cross-references, nav entries and a paragraph explaining
# itself. The cap was making the documentation worse in order to satisfy a rule written for code.
# Splits that survive are the ones with a reason of their own - a self-contained argument, or two
# branches editing one file - and those reasons are visible without a gate.
docs/*.md

# Vendored third-party source: the allocator's C and the two crates that wrap it. Upstream's
# file lengths are not ours to budget, and pinning individual paths would mean editing this
# list on every version bump. `VENDOR.md` records what is vendored, from where, and why.
vendor/**

[warn]

# Empty on purpose: every hand-written file in this repo is under the limit. An entry here
# is a promise to split a file, not a way to silence the gate - and it keeps printing until
# somebody keeps that promise.

# Scratch a browser tool writes into the working tree: verbatim dumps of visited pages, thousands
# of lines each, regenerated on every run and gitignored. Generated output by any reading, and the
# reason it is listed rather than left to fail is that a developer who used that tool could not
# then pass the gates - the same shape as the `site/` exemption above.
.playwright-mcp/**
